1. PURPOSE AND SCOPE
This policy establishes standards for identifying, managing, and reporting financial conflicts of interest (FCOI) to ensure the integrity of research, programs, and operations at the Ronin Institute for Independent Scholarship 2.0. This policy applies to all Investigators, key personnel, and employees involved in the design, conduct, or reporting of research or program activities.
This policy is designed to comply with applicable federal regulations, including but not limited to the Public Health Service (PHS) Policy on Financial Conflict of Interest (42 CFR Part 50, Subpart F (see https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-I/subchapter-D/part-50/subpart-F), and may be updated as necessary to meet specific funding agency requirements.
2. DEFINITIONS
Financial Conflict of Interest (FCOI): A significant financial interest that could directly and significantly affect the design, conduct, or reporting of research or other organizational activities.
Financial Interest: Anything of monetary value received or held by an Investigator or their family members, including but not limited to:
* Salary, consulting fees, honoraria, or other payments
* Equity interests (stocks, stock options, or other ownership interests)
* Intellectual property rights and interests
* Travel reimbursements and expenses
Significant Financial Interest (SFI): A financial interest that meets one or more of the following criteria:
* For publicly traded entities: aggregate value exceeding $5,000 in the 12 months preceding disclosure
* For non-publicly traded entities: any amount of remuneration or equity interest
* Intellectual property rights and interests upon receipt of income exceeding $5,000 in the 12 months preceding disclosure
* Reimbursed or sponsored travel exceeding $5,000 in the 12 months preceding disclosure
Investigator: Any individual responsible for the design, conduct, or reporting of research or other covered activities, including principal investigators, co-investigators, and other key personnel.
Family Members: Spouse, domestic partner, and dependent children.
Institution: Ronin Institute for Independent Scholarship 2.0 (AKA RIIS 2.0) and its subsidiaries or affiliates.
3. DISCLOSURE REQUIREMENTS
3.1 Annual Disclosure
All Investigators must submit annual financial disclosure statements no later than the first business day on or after 15 April of each year, disclosing all significant financial interests held by themselves and their family members.
3.2 Ongoing Disclosure
Investigators must update their disclosure within 30 days of:
* Acquiring new significant financial interests
* Changes to existing financial interests that create or alter potential conflicts
* Beginning new research projects or activities
3.3 Travel Disclosure
Investigators must disclose reimbursed or sponsored travel related to their institutional responsibilities, including:
* Purpose of trip
* Identity of sponsor/organizer
* Destination and duration
* Monetary value (if known)
3.4 Disclosure Form
All disclosures must be submitted using a form substantially similar to the RIIS 2.0 standard disclosure form template and include sufficient detail to enable conflict assessment. A template of the required form is appended to this policy as Appendix A
4. REVIEW AND DETERMINATION PROCESS
4.1 Responsible Official
The Council of RIIS 2.0 shall elect a person to serve as the Responsible Official of RIIS 2.0 for FCOI matters (AKA “Responsible Official”) by simple majority vote, and the Responsible Official may delegate review responsibilities to one or more qualified individuals or committees. In the case of a vacancy in the position of Responsible Official, the Secretary of RIIS 2.0 shall serve as interim Responsible Official.
4.2 Review Timeline
* Initial review of disclosures must be completed within 30 days of receipt
* Determinations regarding conflicts of interest must be made within 60 days of disclosure
4.3 Conflict Determination
The Responsible Official will determine whether a significant financial interest constitutes a financial conflict of interest by assessing whether the interest could directly and significantly affect the design, conduct, or reporting of research or other institutional activities.
4.4 Documentation
All reviews, determinations, and management decisions must be documented in writing and maintained in RIIS 2.0 records in a manner specified by the Secretary of RIIS 2.0.
5. MANAGEMENT OF CONFLICTS
5.1 Management Plan
When a financial conflict of interest is identified, RIIS 2.0 will develop and implement a management plan before research or related activities commence. Management strategies may include:
* Disclosure of the conflict to relevant parties
* Monitoring of research or activities by independent reviewers
* Modification of the research plan or activities
* Disqualification from participation in affected activities
* Divestiture of significant financial interests
* Severance of relationships that create financial conflicts of interest
5.2 Monitoring and Compliance
RIIS 2.0 will monitor compliance with management plans throughout the duration of relevant activities and conduct periodic reviews to ensure continued effectiveness.
5.3 Failure to Comply
Failure to comply with this policy may result in disciplinary action up to and including termination of affiliation, employment, or contract, and may require retrospective review of affected activities.
6. REPORTING REQUIREMENTS
6.1 External Reporting
When required by funding agencies or regulations, RIIS 2.0 will submit FCOI reports within specified timeframes and update reports as circumstances change.
6.2 Public Accessibility
When required by applicable regulations, RIIS 2.0 will make information about financial conflicts of interest publicly accessible through appropriate mechanisms.
7. TRAINING AND EDUCATION
7.1 Required Training
All Investigators must complete FCOI training:
* Prior to engaging in research or covered activities
* At least every four years thereafter
* When this policy is substantively updated
* When an Investigator is found to be non-compliant
7.2 Training Content
Training will address this policy, applicable regulations, responsibilities for disclosure, and RIIS 2.0's review process. Absent training requirements determined by the Responsible Official to be more appropriate, the training content shall be as specified by the National Institutes of Health at https://grants.nih.gov/policy-and-compliance/policy-topics/fcoi/fcoi-training
8. RECORD RETENTION
RIIS 2.0 will maintain records related to financial conflicts of interest for at least seven years after final expenditure report submission or, for ongoing projects, until completion of the project, whichever is longer.
9. APPEALS PROCESS
Investigators may appeal conflict determinations or management decisions by submitting a written appeal to the Council within 30 days of notification. The appeal will be reviewed by individuals without conflicts of interest related to the matter.
10. INSTITUTIONAL CONFLICTS
10.1 Scope
This policy also addresses institutional financial conflicts of interest that may arise from RIIS 2.0's own financial interests in research or other activities.
10.2 Management
Institutional conflicts will be managed through appropriate mechanisms, which may include independent oversight, disclosure, or other measures as determined by the Council.
11. POLICY ADMINISTRATION
11.1 Policy Updates
This policy will be reviewed annually and updated as necessary to comply with applicable regulations and best practices.
11.2 Questions and Guidance
Questions about this policy should be directed to a Council member or other designated Responsible Official at 
11.3 Effective 
This policy is effective as of 2025-07-22 and supersedes all previous Financial Conflict of Interest policies.
